"[2601.09913v1] Continuum Memory Architectures for Long-Horizon LLM Agents (https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.09913v1)\nciteturn30891view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.09913v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 162\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Artificial Intelligence\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 14 Jan 2026]\nL9: # Title:Continuum Memory Architectures for Long-Horizon LLM Agents\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Joe Logan L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Continuum Memory Architectures for Long-Horizon LLM Agents, by Joe Logan\nL14: \nL15: cite6†View PDF cite7†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) has become the default strategy for providing large language model (LLM) agents with contextual knowledge. Yet RAG treats memory as a stateless lookup table: information persists indefinitely, retrieval is read-only, and temporal continuity is absent.\nL17: We define the \\textit{Continuum Memory Architecture} (CMA), a class of systems that maintain and update internal state across interactions through persistent storage, selective retention, associative routing, temporal chaining, and consolidation into higher-order abstractions.\nL18: Rather than disclosing implementation specifics, we specify the architectural requirements CMA imposes and show consistent behavioral advantages on tasks that expose RAG's structural inability to accumulate, mutate, or disambiguate memory.\nL19: The empirical probes (knowledge updates, temporal association, associative recall, contextual disambiguation) demonstrate that CMA is a necessary architectural primitive for long-horizon agents while highlighting open challenges around latency, drift, and interpretability.\nL20: Comments:  | 10 Pages\nL21: Subjects:  | Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Information Retrieval (cs.IR)\nL22: Cite as:  | cite8†arXiv:2601.09913 [cs.AI]\nL23:    | (or cite9†arXiv:2601.09913v1 [cs.AI] for this version)\nL24:    | cite10†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2601.09913†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Joe Logan [cite11†view email ]\nL28: [v1] Wed, 14 Jan 2026 22:40:35 UTC (124 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled Continuum Memory Architectures for Long-Horizon LLM Agents, by Joe Logan\nL35: \nL36:   * cite6†View PDF L37:   * cite7†HTML (experimental) L38:   * cite12†TeX Source L39: \nL40: cite13†view license L41: \nL42: ### Current browse context:\nL43: \nL44: cs.AI\nL45: \nL46: cite14†< prev |   cite15†next > L47: \nL48: cite16†new | cite17†recent | cite18†2026-01 L49: \nL50: Change to browse by:\nL51: \nL52: cite19†cs L53: cite20†cs.IR L54: ### References & Citations\nL55: \nL56:   * cite21†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L57:   * cite22†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L58:   * cite23†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L59: \nL60: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL61: \nL62: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL63: \nL64: [Button: ×]\nL65: \nL66: loading...\nL67: \nL68: Data provided by:\nL69: \nL70: ### Bookmark\nL71: \nL72: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL73: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL74: \nL75: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL76: \nL77: Bibliographic Explorer (cite24†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL78: \nL79: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL80: \nL81: Connected Papers (cite25†What is Connected Papers?†www.connectedpapers.com )\nL82: \nL83: [Input] Litmaps Toggle\nL84: \nL85: Litmaps (cite26†What is Litmaps?†www.litmaps.co )\nL86: \nL87: [Input] scite.ai Toggle\nL88: \nL89: scite Smart Ci